        "name": "The Atlas Key Rule",
        "body": "Pink means measured, yellow means yours, stone means estimated, and these never carry other meanings. If a color is not saying where a number came from, it is the wrong color. The logo and drawn atlas plates may use all three, because they are drawings of the key.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Yellow Step Rule",
        "body": "A screen has exactly one yellow next step. Everything else clickable is atlas, white or a plain link. If a whole closing band is yellow, that band is the step and its button goes ink.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Separate Verdict Rule",
        "body": "A verdict speaks only in green (#1e7a46), red (#b3261e) or ink (#0f1f3d). Verdict color never borrows from the key, and the key never signals good or bad.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Printed Strip Rule",
        "body": "Headlines on the live map are printed strips: each line its own map-sheet band with an ink outline, stacked 1px over the last, sized to its text. The city stays visible between the words and every word stays readable. Never put display type on the moving map without its strip.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Shot Rule",
        "body": "The landing story is one continuous camera shot over the real street. There are no cuts: the camera descends on load, then the scroll scrubs it through every scene, with a slow drift so the city is alive when the page is still. Words change, the map never reloads. Changing the address flies the same camera to the new street.",
        "section": "layout"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Flat Print Rule",
        "body": "No shadows, and no gradient as a surface. A pattern that prints, like a hatch marking a zone on a chart, is ink and stays allowed.",
